Agent team bus: joining a team as an `lxc` member
=================================================

An `lxc` member is a Claude Code agent running inside an LXC container. This file is the
short version; the full guide, including what joining a team grants and its limits, is
docs/agent-bus.md in the fedora-desktop repository.

REQUIRED: gh in the container, logged in to github.com as the agent user. pingbus checks
every reference at GitHub with gh's login and nothing else; without it `pingbus config
check` and `agent-bus-claude` refuse (exit 78): "gh is required and must be logged in:
install gh and run `gh auth login --hostname github.com`". Setting this up is yours,
whether the member is joined by hand or by the play below; nothing here does it.

On a fedora-desktop host, declare the member in `agent_bus_lxc_members` instead and run
play-agent-bus.yml: it runs these steps (helpers/agent_bus_lxc/members.py), adding a
fixed `--seat` so the handle stays the same, leaves a member already joined alone, and
refuses with what to fix. It needs `runuser` and GNU `env` (coreutils 8.28 or later) in
the container, and checks gh's login for the agent user in every container before it
changes anything, stopping with the container's name when it is missing. A member joined by hand keeps its numbered seat: declare it with `seat: <n>`.
A repository that makes the containers itself: docs/agent-bus-lxc-members.md in the
fedora-desktop repository lists what it must provide and how its sessions start.
Elsewhere, by hand:

1. The kit inside the container. Copy this kit, /usr/local/share/agent-bus/kit/, to the
   same path inside the container and link its `pingbus` and `agent-bus-claude` into
   /usr/local/bin. The container needs Python 3.11 or later; `pingbus` refuses an older
   one (exit 78).

2. The network. The container reaches the homeserver host's bus address, `<bus_ip>`,
   routed through the LXC bridge, so the team file's `allow_from` must list the bridge's
   subnet. A container on another machine reaches the team over WireGuard instead.

3. The member's arguments. Inside the container, as the agent user, in its checkout:

   ```
   HOOKS_DAEMON_HOSTNAME=<role> pingbus suggest-handle
   ```

   prints `--repo=... --host=... --type=lxc`.

4. The bundle. On the team's homeserver host, a human with sudo runs:

   ```
   sudo agent-bus add-member <team> --repo=<repo> --host=<role> --type=lxc --role=worker --address=<bus_ip> --out=$HOME/bundle-<team>
   ```

   (`--no-human-text` for pings only). Copy the bundle into the container as
   ~<agent-user>/.config/pingbus/<team>/, owned by the agent user, directory 0700 and
   files 0600, and delete the copy left behind.

5. The env file, ~<agent-user>/.config/pingbus/env inside the container:

   ```
   PINGBUS_TEAMS=<team>
   HOOKS_DAEMON_HOSTNAME=<role>
   ```

6. Check and start. As the agent user, `pingbus config check` must print no error; then
   start sessions with `agent-bus-claude`. The agent user must not hold a human's Matrix
   session, here as anywhere.
